# Content-Security-Policy for the static deploy (Cloudflare Pages _headers).
#
# - script-src does NOT need 'unsafe-eval', and must not regain it. Nothing the
#   app bundles uses eval at all: the codec's deflate/inflate is `pako`, plain
#   ESM with no eval and no asm.js. Verified per build - `grep 'eval(' dist/`
#   over the emitted bundle finds nothing.
#
#   'wasm-unsafe-eval' IS present, and it is a DIFFERENT token (#222). It permits
#   WebAssembly compilation and instantiation and nothing else - no `eval`, no
#   `new Function`, no inline script. The noise engine
#   (`src/noise/wasm/engine.wasm`) needs it; without it `WebAssembly.compile`
#   throws a CSP error and the engine cannot start.
#
#   The two tokens look alike and the older guard could not tell them apart: it
#   asserted the policy did not CONTAIN the substring `unsafe-eval`, which
#   `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` also contains. `test/buildStamp.spec.ts` now splits
#   script-src on whitespace and compares whole tokens, so it rejects
#   `'unsafe-eval'` and REQUIRES `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` - the second half matters
#   too, because dropping the narrow token would break the app rather than
#   loosen it, which is a failure nobody would read as a security change.
#
#   This line used to carry a much longer caveat, because the codec was backed
#   by `zlib-asm`, an asm.js port that shipped three eval sites (2016 Emscripten
#   runtime glue, none of them compression) and needed a local patch to strip
#   them so this policy could drop 'unsafe-eval'. That dependency and its patch
#   are gone; the constraint they were working around no longer exists.
#
#   VERIFIED LIVE 2026-08-19 against deploy e723b30, and how that check was
#   built matters more than the fact it came back clean. THIS FILE ONLY TAKES
#   EFFECT ON CLOUDFLARE PAGES, so the policy had never run in production until
#   that deploy - which is also the first one carrying the WASM engine, i.e. the
#   first thing here that needs 'wasm-unsafe-eval' at all. Four parts, in this
#   order, because a run with no violations proves nothing on its own:
#
#   1. CALIBRATION. `eval("1+1")` and `new Function(...)` were tried in the
#      measuring context FIRST. Both threw EvalError naming the CSP. A debugger
#      context that bypassed page CSP would have made every probe below pass for
#      the wrong reason, so this is what licenses reading the rest - and it is
#      also the direct evidence that 'unsafe-eval' is not in force.
#   2. CONTROL, which must FIRE. An inline <script> inserted through the DOM
#      never executed, and recorded violatedDirective "script-src-elem",
#      blockedURI "inline".
#   3. POSITIVE PROBE, for the half that breaks the app rather than loosening
#      it. The shipped engine-*.wasm (application/wasm, 84,177 bytes, sha256
#      cd1a79c1..., byte-identical to src/noise/wasm/engine.wasm) compiled AND
#      instantiated in the page world, all 28 exports present.
#   4. NORMAL OPERATION, with a securitypolicyviolation listener installed
#      before any page script ran: server preview (so connect-src is right), a
#      client-side Fulgora terrain render at 1024x1024 across 11 workers, and a
#      radius-1024 island search. Zero violations, zero console errors.
#
#   This paragraph used to say the policy "was verified live in Chrome under the
#   zlib-asm build", with all 9 builtin fixtures re-encoded and an inline script
#   blocked. That was true when written and had quietly stopped describing
#   anything that ships: pako replaced zlib-asm, and the WASM engine arrived
#   after both, so the run being cited had never exercised the token this rule
#   now turns on. Re-verify after any deploy that changes what the app compiles
#   or fetches, and name the build the run was against.
# - img-src needs blob:: the preview PNG is shown via URL.createObjectURL(blob).
# - connect-src must include the preview Worker origin (the app's only outbound
#   call). Replace the placeholder with the deployed Worker origin.
# - style-src 'unsafe-inline' covers runtime-injected component styles.
# - script-src allows static.cloudflareinsights.com: Cloudflare AUTO-INJECTS its
#   Web Analytics beacon into proxied Pages responses, and the old policy blocked
#   it, so analytics silently recorded nothing and every page load logged two CSP
#   console errors (Lighthouse Best Practices 92 in production, 100 locally,
#   because nothing is injected on localhost).
#
#   The origin is listed WITHOUT a path on purpose. Cloudflare's docs suggest
#   `https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js`, but the injected URL
#   carries a version segment (.../beacon.min.js/v4513226cd...), and a CSP source
#   with a path that does not end in `/` must match the path EXACTLY - so the
#   documented value would not match the thing actually being loaded, and would
#   break again on every beacon version bump.
#
#   No connect-src entry is needed. Under automatic injection the beacon reports
#   to this site's own /cdn-cgi/rum, which 'self' already covers; the separate
#   cloudflareinsights.com endpoint applies only to manual embedding.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self' https://fmw-preview.wormeyman.workers.dev; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com

# /version.json carries the git build stamp and exists to answer "did my deploy
# land?" (`pnpm run verify:deploy`). A cached copy would answer that question
# about the PREVIOUS deploy while looking authoritative, which is worse than no
# answer at all - so it is the one path here that must never be served stale.
#
# Unlike the hashed bundles, its URL is CONSTANT across deploys, so the edge has
# no cache key change to notice; `no-store` is what stops it being reused. The
# checker also sends a unique query string and `cache: no-store`, but belt and
# braces is deliberate: the browser tab a human opens to eyeball the file sends
# neither.
#
# This rule sets no Content-Security-Policy, so the `/*` policy above still
# applies to this path unchanged. Nothing here weakens it.
/version.json
  Cache-Control: no-store
